# ─── SECURITY: never commit these ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Internal audit + operations docs. The QA report enumerates unfixed exploitable
# vulnerabilities with working repro commands and the production project ref.
# This must never reach a public repo, and it is the reason this repo is private.
# Scoped to .md on purpose: a bare *REMEDIATION* also matched
# lib/__tests__/remediation-invariants.test.ts (git uses core.ignorecase on macOS),
# silently dropping a real test file from the repo.
#
# `/docs/*` (not `/docs/`) so the allowlist below can work at all: a gitignore
# negation cannot re-include a file whose PARENT DIRECTORY is excluded. Everything
# in docs/ stays ignored by default; the runbooks below are deliberate exceptions
# and are operational procedure, not vulnerability disclosure. Adding a new
# committed doc means adding a line here — `git add -f` works but leaves the next
# person's `git status` lying to them.
/docs/*
!/docs/enterprise-sso-runbook.md
!/docs/email-deliverability.md
# Committed on purpose: procurement teams ask for the audit behind the public
# conformance statement, and a statement with no auditable record behind it is
# a claim, not evidence. It lists accessibility gaps, not security ones.
!/docs/accessibility-audit.md
*QA-REPORT*.md
*QA-FINDINGS*.md
*REMEDIATION*.md
*HANDOFF*.md

# secrets, keys, credentials
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*.crt
*.jks
*.keystore
# Root-anchored on purpose: a bare `credentials.*` also matches the real source
# file app/actions/credentials.ts. It survives today only because it was tracked
# before this pattern existed — untrack it once and it silently disappears.
/secrets.*
/credentials.*
/service-account*.json
*.jwt
*.sid

# local scratch / agent working files
/scratchpad/
/.scratch/
*.local.md
.claude/settings.local.json

# database dumps + backups (contain real user data)
*.dump
*.sql.gz
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
/backups/
/supabase/.dump/

# uploaded artifacts (coach photo IDs, team media)
/uploads/
/tmp/
